1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,637
This is the surface of the western
Pacific, above the Mariana Trench.

2
00:00:06,132 --> 00:00:09,838
The bottom is almost eleven
kilometres straight down.

3
00:00:10,730 --> 00:00:12,169
Let's fall.

4
00:00:14,255 --> 00:00:15,401
The sunlight zone.

5
00:00:15,401 --> 00:00:17,949
The top two hundred metres of the ocean,

6
00:00:17,949 --> 00:00:22,270
where there is enough light for
drifting plant life to grow.

7
00:00:22,964 --> 00:00:28,270
Every ten metres of water adds roughly
one more atmosphere of pressure.

8
00:00:28,964 --> 00:00:33,497
Forty metres. The limit for
recreational scuba divers.

9
00:00:34,190 --> 00:00:38,809
Red light has already been absorbed.
Everything is turning blue.

10
00:00:40,298 --> 00:00:43,428
Two hundred metres. The twilight zone.

11
00:00:43,923 --> 00:00:46,460
Two hundred and fifty-three metres.

12
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:50,012
The deepest a person has
gone on a single breath:

13
00:00:50,012 --> 00:00:53,992
Herbert Nitsch, riding a
weighted sled, in 2012.

14
00:00:55,084 --> 00:01:00,852
Down here, the light is too faint for
anything to grow. So animals make their own.

15
00:01:01,346 --> 00:01:06,102
Lanternfish glow along their bellies,
matching the dim light from above,

16
00:01:06,102 --> 00:01:09,641
so that hunters below
can't see their shadows.

17
00:01:10,733 --> 00:01:14,147
Somewhere in this zone
lives the giant squid.

18
00:01:14,147 --> 00:01:20,033
It was first filmed in the deep in
2012, about six hundred metres down.

19
00:01:21,124 --> 00:01:23,486
Eight hundred and twenty-eight metres.

20
00:01:23,486 --> 00:01:28,026
The world's tallest building would
now be completely under water.

21
00:01:29,118 --> 00:01:34,061
And at around a thousand metres,
the last trace of sunlight is gone.

22
00:01:34,953 --> 00:01:38,682
From here on, it is night.
It has always been night.

23
00:01:40,967 --> 00:01:42,162
The midnight zone.

24
00:01:42,162 --> 00:01:46,080
The pressure is a hundred times
what it was at the surface,

25
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:49,966
and the water is only a
few degrees above freezing.

26
00:01:50,859 --> 00:01:56,239
Yet about three quarters of the animals
seen in these dark waters can make light.

27
00:01:56,734 --> 00:01:59,833
A jellyfish flashes a spinning ring of blue.

28
00:01:59,833 --> 00:02:02,580
A burglar alarm, some scientists think,

29
00:02:02,580 --> 00:02:07,377
to call in bigger predators
against whatever is attacking it.

30
00:02:08,269 --> 00:02:14,911
An anglerfish dangles a glowing lure: a fin
spine, tipped with light-making bacteria.

31
00:02:15,605 --> 00:02:21,956
A gulper eel drifts by, almost all mouth,
with a tiny light at the tip of its tail.

32
00:02:23,047 --> 00:02:29,206
Two kilometres. Sperm whales dive this
deep to hunt squid, on a single breath.

33
00:02:30,298 --> 00:02:33,858
And if Mount Everest stood
on the floor of this trench,

34
00:02:33,858 --> 00:02:36,253
its summit would be right about here.

35
00:02:36,253 --> 00:02:38,760
Two kilometres below the waves.

36
00:02:39,254 --> 00:02:43,716
We still have almost nine
kilometres of mountain to fall past.

37
00:02:45,206 --> 00:02:47,614
Three thousand eight hundred metres.

38
00:02:47,614 --> 00:02:51,794
The wreck of the Titanic.
Only a third of the way down.

39
00:02:53,681 --> 00:02:55,317
Four thousand metres.

40
00:02:55,317 --> 00:02:56,096
The abyss.

41
00:02:56,096 --> 00:03:02,749
Much of the deep ocean floor lies around
here: four, five, six kilometres down.

42
00:03:03,243 --> 00:03:06,510
But beneath us, the floor has fallen away.

43
00:03:07,403 --> 00:03:10,561
A dumbo octopus, flapping its ear-like fins.

44
00:03:10,561 --> 00:03:14,578
They have been seen almost
seven kilometres down.

45
00:03:16,465 --> 00:03:22,737
Six thousand metres. The hadal zone,
named after Hades, the Greek underworld.

46
00:03:23,431 --> 00:03:27,736
Trenches form where one of Earth's
plates sinks beneath another.

47
00:03:27,736 --> 00:03:32,945
Here, the Pacific plate is sliding,
very slowly, down into the planet.

48
00:03:34,434 --> 00:03:39,019
Eight thousand metres. Snailfish:
small, pale, soft-bodied.

49
00:03:39,019 --> 00:03:45,082
In 2022, one was filmed at eight thousand
three hundred and thirty-six metres.

50
00:03:45,082 --> 00:03:47,680
The deepest fish ever seen.

51
00:03:48,374 --> 00:03:52,035
Not far below, scientists
think, no fish can survive.

52
00:03:52,035 --> 00:03:57,785
The chemistry that protects their cells
from the pressure reaches its limit.

53
00:03:59,871 --> 00:04:05,567
In 1960, Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh
came down here in the bathyscaphe Trieste.

54
00:04:05,567 --> 00:04:08,451
On the way, a window cracked with a bang.

55
00:04:08,451 --> 00:04:12,889
They kept going, and spent
twenty minutes on the bottom.

56
00:04:13,781 --> 00:04:20,973
Nobody returned for fifty-two years. Then,
in 2012, James Cameron came down alone.

57
00:04:21,866 --> 00:04:26,024
And from 2019, Victor Vescovo
dived here again and again,

58
00:04:26,024 --> 00:04:32,360
in a submersible built to be used over
and over, and he brought others with him.

59
00:04:34,645 --> 00:04:39,926
Ten thousand, nine hundred and
thirty-five metres, give or take a few.

60
00:04:40,421 --> 00:04:45,165
The bottom of Challenger Deep.
The deepest known place on Earth.

61
00:04:46,256 --> 00:04:50,779
The pressure is about eleven hundred
times what it is at the surface.

62
00:04:50,779 --> 00:04:54,228
More than a tonne on
every square centimetre.

63
00:04:54,922 --> 00:04:59,657
The water is about two degrees. And
no sunlight has ever reached it.

64
00:05:00,947 --> 00:05:02,814
And still, there is life.

65
00:05:02,814 --> 00:05:06,623
Amphipods, scavengers
related to beach sandhoppers,

66
00:05:06,623 --> 00:05:10,259
swarm over anything that sinks from above.

67
00:05:12,544 --> 00:05:17,500
For half a century, more people had walked
on the Moon than had ever come here.

68
00:05:17,500 --> 00:05:19,443
That is no longer true.

69
00:05:20,137 --> 00:05:26,508
Dozens have now made the dive. One of them,
Kathy Sullivan, had also walked in space.

70
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:33,256
But we have still sent far more people into
space than to the bottom of our own ocean.

71
00:05:35,343 --> 00:05:40,157
Eleven kilometres. Less than a
ten-minute drive, if it were a road.

72
00:05:41,248 --> 00:05:44,000
And almost no one has ever been here.

